CAT = "cfal1Fsa"; SEG = "FSA"
Q = [
("Under US GAAP, interest paid on a company's debt is classified in the statement of cash flows as:",
 "Operating activities", ["Financing activities", "Investing activities", "Operating or financing, by choice"],
 "US GAAP puts interest paid in operating activities. IAS 7 has let IFRS reporters choose operating or financing, and the IAS 7 amendments that come with IFRS 18 (periods from 1 January 2027) put it in financing for most companies, so check the basis before comparing cash flow from operations."),
("Under US GAAP, dividends a company receives from its investments are classified in the statement of cash flows as:",
 "Operating activities", ["Investing activities", "Financing activities", "Operating or investing, by choice"],
 "US GAAP treats dividends and interest received as operating cash flows. IAS 7 has allowed IFRS reporters to show them in operating or investing activities, and with IFRS 18 (from 2027) most companies show them in investing."),
("Which model for measuring property, plant and equipment after acquisition is allowed under IFRS but not under US GAAP?",
 "The revaluation model", ["The cost model", "Straight-line depreciation", "The units-of-production method"],
 "IFRS permits carrying PP&E at fair value less later depreciation and impairment (the revaluation model). US GAAP requires the cost model."),
("A US GAAP company reports LIFO ending inventory of 260 and a LIFO reserve of 40. To compare it with a FIFO company, its ending inventory restated to FIFO is:",
 "300", ["220", "260", "340"],
 "FIFO inventory = LIFO inventory + LIFO reserve = 260 + 40 = 300. Subtracting the reserve gives 220, and 340 adds it twice. IFRS prohibits LIFO, so this adjustment is also needed to compare a LIFO company with an IFRS reporter."),
("Under IFRS, an earlier write-down of inventory to net realizable value may later be:",
 "Reversed, up to original cost", ["Reversed above original cost", "Never reversed", "Reversed only at year-end audit"],
 "IFRS requires reversal of a write-down when NRV recovers, capped at the amount originally written down. US GAAP does not allow reversals."),
("In a period of rising prices with stable inventory levels, a LIFO company compared with an identical FIFO company reports:",
 "Higher inventory turnover", ["Higher gross margin", "Higher ending inventory", "Higher current ratio"],
 "LIFO puts the newest, higher costs in cost of goods sold and older, lower costs in inventory. Higher COGS over lower inventory gives higher turnover, and lower inventory lowers the current ratio."),
("A company capitalizes a cost rather than expensing it. In the year the cost is paid, cash flow from operations is:",
 "Higher, with the outflow in investing", ["Lower, with the outflow in operating", "Unchanged in every section", "Higher, with no outflow reported"],
 "A capitalized cost is an investing outflow, so CFO is higher than if it were expensed. Total cash flow is the same either way."),
("A company has a net profit margin of 5%, total asset turnover of 1.6 and financial leverage (assets to equity) of 2.0. Its return on equity is:",
 "16.0%", ["8.0%", "10.0%", "3.2%"],
 "DuPont: ROE = margin x turnover x leverage = 0.05 x 1.6 x 2.0 = 16.0%. 8.0% is return on assets, which leaves out leverage."),
("A company has days of sales outstanding of 45, days of inventory on hand of 60 and days of payables of 30. Its cash conversion cycle is:",
 "75 days", ["45 days", "105 days", "135 days"],
 "Cash conversion cycle = DSO + DOH - DPO = 45 + 60 - 30 = 75 days. 135 days adds payables instead of subtracting them."),
("Cash flow from operations is 500, interest paid is 40 (reported in operating activities), the tax rate is 25% and fixed capital investment is 200. Free cash flow to the firm is:",
 "330", ["300", "340", "260"],
 "FCFF = CFO + interest x (1 - t) - FCInv = 500 + 30 - 200 = 330. Adding the pre-tax interest gives 340; leaving it out gives 300."),
("A company uses accelerated depreciation for tax purposes and straight-line in its financial statements. This creates a:",
 "Deferred tax liability", ["Deferred tax asset", "Permanent difference", "Valuation allowance"],
 "Higher tax depreciation now means lower taxable income than accounting income, so taxes are deferred to later years: a deferred tax liability."),
("Under IFRS 16, a lessee with a five-year office lease recognizes on its balance sheet:",
 "A right-of-use asset and a lease liability", ["Rent expense with no asset or liability", "Only a lease liability, no asset", "Only a right-of-use asset, no liability"],
 "IFRS 16 uses a single lessee model: apart from short-term and low-value leases, every lease brings a right-of-use asset and a lease liability, with depreciation and interest expense."),
("Under IFRS, the recoverable amount used to test an asset for impairment is the:",
 "Higher of FV less costs and value in use", ["Lower of fair value and carrying amount", "Sum of undiscounted future cash flows", "Original cost less accumulated depreciation"],
 "IAS 36 compares carrying amount with recoverable amount, the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use. The undiscounted cash flow test is the US GAAP recoverability step."),
("Under IFRS, costs incurred during the development phase of an internal project are:",
 "Capitalized once criteria are met", ["Always expensed as incurred", "Capitalized from the research phase", "Charged directly to equity"],
 "IAS 38 expenses research costs but capitalizes development costs once feasibility, intent, ability to use or sell and other criteria are met. US GAAP generally expenses both."),
("Accounts receivable have grown much faster than revenue for several years. An analyst should view this as a possible sign of:",
 "Aggressive revenue recognition", ["Conservative revenue recognition", "Improving collection efficiency", "Lower credit risk"],
 "Receivables outpacing sales suggest revenue recorded before cash is likely, such as looser credit terms or channel stuffing, a classic earnings quality warning."),
("A company ships extra goods to distributors near quarter-end to meet sales targets, with generous return rights. This practice is known as:",
 "Channel stuffing", ["Big bath accounting", "Cookie jar reserves", "Window dressing of cash"],
 "Channel stuffing pulls future sales into the current period. Big bath accounting takes large losses in one bad period to flatter later ones."),
("An auditor concludes that the financial statements are presented fairly in all material respects. The auditor issues:",
 "An unmodified opinion", ["A qualified opinion", "An adverse opinion", "A disclaimer of opinion"],
 "An unmodified (clean) opinion means no material misstatement was found. A qualified opinion has an exception, an adverse opinion finds pervasive misstatement, and a disclaimer means no opinion could be formed."),
("In a vertical common-size income statement, each line item is expressed as a percentage of:",
 "Revenue", ["Total assets", "Net income", "Shareholders' equity"],
 "Vertical common-size analysis divides every income statement line by revenue, so margins can be compared across companies of different sizes. Balance sheet lines are divided by total assets."),
("Under US GAAP, inventory measured using FIFO or weighted average cost is carried at the:",
 "Lower of cost and net realizable value", ["Lower of cost or market", "Higher of cost and fair value", "Current replacement cost"],
 "Since ASU 2015-11, US GAAP measures FIFO and average-cost inventory at the lower of cost and NRV, as IFRS does for all inventory. The older lower of cost or market rule, which uses replacement cost, remains only for LIFO and the retail method."),
("A bond is issued at a discount. Using the effective interest method, interest expense each period is:",
 "More than the coupon paid", ["Less than the coupon paid", "Equal to the coupon paid", "Zero until maturity"],
 "Interest expense equals the market rate at issue times the carrying amount, and the market rate exceeds the coupon rate for a discount bond. The difference amortizes the discount."),
("Revenue is 800 and cost of sales is 520. The gross profit margin is:",
 "35%", ["65%", "54%", "28%"],
 "Gross margin = (800 - 520) / 800 = 280 / 800 = 35%. 65% is cost of sales as a share of revenue, and 54% divides gross profit by cost of sales."),
("A company's effective tax rate is well below its statutory rate year after year. The most likely cause is:",
 "Permanent differences", ["Accelerated tax depreciation", "A growing deferred tax liability", "Expected future rate cuts"],
 "Temporary differences shift when tax is paid but leave the total tax expense at the statutory rate. Permanent differences, such as tax-exempt income, change the effective rate itself."),
("Under IFRS, goodwill acquired in a business combination is:",
 "Tested for impairment yearly", ["Amortized over ten years", "Expensed in the year acquired", "Revalued upward to fair value"],
 "Goodwill is not amortized under IFRS. It is allocated to cash-generating units and tested for impairment every year, and impairment losses on goodwill are never reversed."),
("A financial covenant in a loan agreement typically requires the borrower to:",
 "Keep a ratio above a set level", ["Pay dividends every quarter", "Issue new shares each year", "Report results to rival firms"],
 "Financial covenants set limits such as a minimum interest coverage or maximum leverage ratio. Breaching one can make the debt due early, so analysts check the headroom."),
]
